#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:36
#: 134b33c1a31a443781a9133e94e79821
msgid "Every platform provides a 1kHz timer in the AP_HAL. Any code in ArduPilot can register a timer function which is then called at 1kHz. All registered timer functions are called sequentially. This very primitive mechanism is used as it is extremely portable, and yet very useful. You register a timer callback by calling the hal.scheduler->register_timer_process() like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:47
#: a8e9b36b4d474b8fa96816a33393f821
msgid "that particular example is from the MS5611 barometer driver. The AP_HAL_MEMBERPROC() macro provides a way to encapsulate a C++ member function as a callback argument (bundling up the object context with the function pointer)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:52
#: 8c0bcac90f3441e79404582c2d5ab48d
msgid "When a piece of code wants something to happen at less than 1kHz then it should maintain its own \"last_called\" variable and return immediately if not enough time has passed. You can use the hal.scheduler->millis() and hal.scheduler->micros() functions to get the time since boot in milliseconds and microseconds to support this."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:58
#: 3f6eec34fdb14cc3b5d6dcb1f217273a
msgid "You should now go and modify an existing example sketch (or create a new one) and add a timer callback. Make the timer increment a counter then print the value of the counter every second in the loop() function. Modify your function so that it increments the counter  every 25 milliseconds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:67
#: b9da0973b9c74c3c9af75452da68ffe4
msgid "On platforms that support real threads the AP_HAL for that platform will create a number of threads to support basic operations. For example, on Pixhawk the following HAL specific threads are created:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:71
#: 229dbcca255b4eada1b4c4e179ddc26b
msgid "The UART thread, for reading and writing UARTs (and USB)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:72
#: 827a5021903a4b5ead047ef6944568ac
msgid "The timer thread, which supports the 1kHz timer functionality described above"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:74
#: 72a3be5df4cc47f3be26e1965a8de2c7
msgid "The IO thread, which supports writing to the microSD card, EEPROM and FRAM"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:77
#: 8bd571c8b96a49d1a6a95a66657aa444
msgid "Have a look in Scheduler.cpp inside each AP_HAL implementation to see what threads are created and what the realtime priority of each thread is."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:81
#: 1a7d6e7c5e644207ad6d8e6265b76afe
msgid "If you have a Pixhawk then you should also now setup a debug console cable and attach it to the nsh console (the serial5 port). Connect at 57600. When you have connected, try the \"ps\" command and you will get something like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:101
#: 87ce196f766545a78a8e54ae84d2b822
msgid "In this example you can see the \"AHRS_Test\" thread, which is running the example sketch from libraries/AP_AHRS/examples/AHRS_Test. You can also see the timer thread (priority 181), the UART thread (priority 60) and the IO thread (priority 59)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:106
#: 36aaedb0676e485f9a9d35eef261a1d4
msgid "Additionally you can see the px4io, fmuservo, uavcan, lpwork, hpwork and idle tasks. More about those later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:109
#: 19cad977e0644855bd70937f28a9b643
msgid "Other AP_HAL ports have more or less threads depending on what is needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:112
#: fa1b4fa0f3a8460a9b6aa15ed261b839
msgid "One common use of threads is to provide drivers a way to schedule slow tasks without interrupting the main autopilot flight code. For example, the AP_Terrain library needs to be able to do file IO to the microSD card (to store and retrieve terrain data). The way it does this is it calls the function hal.scheduler->register_io_process() like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:122
#: a545322d75fc470b9b6528f89f0a20ce
msgid "The sets up the AP_Terrain::io_timer function to be called regularly. That is called within the boards IO thread, meaning it is a low realtime priority and is suitable for storage IO tasks. It is important that slow IO tasks like this not be called on the timer thread as they would cause delays in the more important processing of high speed sensor data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:129
#: e7623843104b457f9e0c630b7d826a19
msgid "Driver specific threads"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:131
#: 28a36b955ba343079c6d09ad2367e97e
msgid "It is also possible to create driver specific threads, to support asynchronous processing in a manner specific to one driver. Currently you can only create driver specific threads in a manner that is platform dependent, so this is only appropriate if your driver is intended to run only on one type of autopilot board. If you want it to run on multiple AP_HAL targets then you have two choices:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:138
#: c72dd6ea65f34cdbb5e25cc8b8f43686
msgid "you can use the register_io_process() and register_timer_process() scheduler calls to use the existing timer or IO threads"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:141
#: 6747779300674a27b77f5a6eb8a3f439
msgid "you can add a new HAL interface that provides a generic way to create threads on multiple AP_HAL targets (please contribute patches back)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:144
#: 1b90001b4f3c48ff9f2d471db9bd1358
msgid "An example of a driver specific thread is the ToneAlarm thread in the Linux port. See AP_HAL_Linux/ToneAlarmDriver.cpp"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:148
#: a51ba325baeb42a2beaa6cba5e0dbae7
msgid "ArduPilot drivers versus platform drivers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:150
#: 5e3fd65d6e754834aa33de3a0ee2b08e
msgid "You may notice some driver duplication in ArduPilot. For example, we have a MPU6000 driver in libraries/AP_InertalSensor/AP_InertialSensor_MPU6000.cpp, and another MPU6000 driver in PX4Firmware/src/drivers/mpu6000."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:155
#: 0b810aa5a5064a0eb2596e86ef7547d6
msgid "The reason for this duplication is that the PX4 project already provides a set of well tested drivers for hardware that comes with Pixhawk boards, and we enjoy a good collaborative relationship with the PX4 team on developing and enhancing these drivers. So when we build ArduPilot for PX4 we take advantage of the PX4 drivers by writing small \"shim\" drivers which present the PX4 drivers with the standard ArduPilot library interface. If you look at libraries/AP_InertialSensor/AP_InertialSensor_PX4.cpp you will see a small shim driver that asks the PX4 what IMU drivers are available on this board and automatically makes all of them available as part of the ArduPilot AP_InertialSensor library."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:167
#: 6bfbb4b737fc4a488c8a398612ea717f
msgid "So if we have an MPU6000 on the board we use the AP_InertialSensor_MPU6000.cpp driver on non-Pixhawk/NuttX platforms, and the AP_InertialSensor_PX4.cpp driver on NuttX based platforms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:171
#: b91289d0a83949278550bf43b8291be2
msgid "The same type of split can also happen for other AP_HAL ports. For example, we could use Linux kernel drivers for some sensors on Linux boards. For other sensors we use the generic AP_HAL I2C and SPI interfaces to use the ArduPilot \"in-tree\" drivers which work across a wide range of boards."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:178
#: 2e6916f531ac49c0a2ee36b3563ea1b6
msgid "Platform specific threads and tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:180
#: 6cbfead10ca748b4b7a5f4a293486ef9
msgid "On some platforms there will be a number of base tasks and threads that will be created by the startup process. These are very platform specific so for the sake of this tutorial I will concentrate on the tasks used on PX4 based boards."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:185
#: ff82eefd600c47c18cf7b5857c8284f1
msgid "In the \"ps\" output above we saw a number of tasks and threads that were not started by the AP_HAL_PX4 Scheduler code. Specifically they are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:188
#: b3de0678becf4402924601ebab6485bf
msgid "idle task - called when there is nothing else to run"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:189
#: be324077629e4c85829e309b3a40e1bc
msgid "init - used to start up the system"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:190
#: 3bcfb5fa44114ebca2f80a484e6450e1
msgid "px4io - handle the communication with the PX4IO co-processor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:191
#: 3419fd47b46944b8a46d1989a4cc6d38
msgid "hpwork - handle thread based PX4 drivers (mainly I2C drivers)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:192
#: 1555f2bf9a2146ddaba0687d60133215
msgid "lpwork - handle thread based low priority work (eg. IO)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:193
#: 68d03a10bc7e4c6aa6db5807d4fc2835
msgid "fmuservo - handle talking to the auxiliary PWM outputs on the FMU"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:194
#: 7fb48fca7bbc43b3a59b8cd3eb8a5f95
msgid "uavcan - handle the uavcan CANBUS protocol"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:196
#: ed0678d5c9864329917721943b5d01d8
msgid "The startup of all of these tasks is controlled by the PX4 specific `rc.APM script <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/mk/PX4/ROMFS/init.d/rc.APM>`__. That script is run when the PX4 boots, and is responsible for detecting what sort of PX4 board we are using then loading the right tasks and drivers for that board. It is a \"nsh\" script, which is similar to a bourne shell script (though nsh is much more primitive)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:203
#: 8d71faf07800442584427591aa15d755
msgid "As an exercise, try editing the rc.APM script and adding some sleep and echo commands. Then upload a new firmware and connect to the debug console while the board is booting. Your echo commands should show up on the console."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:208
#: 5b0f20db4f244e33acb518675b9ad93a
msgid "Another very useful way of exploring the startup of the PX4 is to boot without a microSD card in the slot. The `rcS script <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/mk/PX4/ROMFS/init.d/rcS>`__, which runs just before rc.APM, detects if a microSD is inserted and gives you a bare nsh console on the USB port if it isn't. You can then manually run all the steps of rc.APM yourself on the USB console to learn how it works."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:215
#: 87b0f9b6abfd41728f456c87d2463c08
msgid "Try the following exercise after booting a Pixhawk without a microSD card and connecting to the USB console:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:229
#: 4d53fc10cf2046e2a36d30e2ddc6edb0
msgid "Try playing with the other drivers. Have a look in /bin to see what is available. The source code for most of these commands is in `PX4Firmware/src/drivers <https://github.com/ArduPilot/PX4Firmware/tree/master/src/drivers>`__. Have a look through the mpu6000 driver to get an idea of what is involved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:235
#: bd2c0d8ff59f433ab632e234d3c412f4
msgid "Given we are on the topic of threads and tasks, a brief description of threads in the PX4Firmware git tree is worth mentioning. If you look in the mpu6000 driver you will see a line like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:243
#: bbdf98a169e54767a0a7264d929f5a17
msgid "that is the equivalent of the hal.scheduler->register_timer_process() function in the AP_HAL, but is PX4 specific and is also much more flexible. It says that it wants the HRT (high resolution timer) subsystem of the PX4 to call the MPU6000::measure_trampoline function every 1000 microseconds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:249
#: 5775d7b6090e4502b3882dbf0d67097d
msgid "Using hrt_call_every() is the common method used for regular events in drivers where the operations are very fast, such as SPI device drivers. The operations are typically run with interrupts disabled, and should take only a few tens of microseconds at most."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:254
#: 522b7be42824444fb648fb61b62b14d7
msgid "If you compare this to the hmc5883 driver, you will instead see a line like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:261
#: e66f9a7ea7084ff380bb580e06857558
msgid "that uses an alternative mechanism for regular events which is suitable for slower devices, such as I2C devices. What this does is add the cycle_trampoline function to a work queue within the hpwork thread that you saw above. Calls made within HPWORK workers should run with interrupts enabled and may take up to a few hundred microseconds. For tasks which will take longer than that the LPWORK work queue should be used, which runs them in the lower priority lpwork thread."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:270
#: 73d5ea37bbda4b10a072211e5ba39e03
msgid "The AP_Scheduler system"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:272
#: d0f28255445e40708a3a84e362018f7d
msgid "The next aspect of ArduPilot threading and tasks to understand is the AP_Scheduler system. The AP_Scheduler library is used to divide up time within the main vehicle thread, while providing some simple mechanisms to control how much time is used for each operation (called a 'task' in AP_Scheduler)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:278
#: 62dfcc5031ae4e4d8cd8359efea05c97
msgid "The way it works is that the loop() function for each vehicle implementation contains some code that does this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:281
#: ba31045dde2e4e15a25e4a650f7643fc
msgid "wait for a new IMU sample to arrive"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:282
#: 999728fff3f64983a661648301277028
msgid "call a set of tasks between each IMU sample"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:284
#: ce29737f49ae47738aed9f84b17f7d18
msgid "It is a table driven scheduler, and each vehicle type has a AP_Scheduler::Task table. To learn how it works have a look at the `AP_Scheduler/examples/Scheduler_test.cpp <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AP_Scheduler/examples/Scheduler_test/Scheduler_test.cpp>`__ sketch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:289
#: b221257aa0d74904a3ade41cfed40b4a
msgid "If you look inside that file you will see a small table with a set of 3 scheduling tasks. Associated with each task are two numbers. The table looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:301
#: 21c374b612464b24935b000917d26146
msgid "The first number after each function name is the call frequency, in units controlled by the ins.init() call. For this example sketch the ins.init() uses RATE_50HZ, so each scheduling step is 20ms. That means the ins_update() call is made every 20ms, the one_hz_print() function is called every 50 times (ie. once a second) and the five_second_call() is called every 250 times (ie. once every 5 seconds)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:309
#: 9ff10ac086cd48c4be44b9e7af475ee6
msgid "The second number is the maximum time that the function is expected to take. This is used to avoid making the call unless there is enough time left in this scheduling run to run the function. When scheduler.run() is called it is passed the amount of time (in microseconds) available for running tasks, and if the worst case time for this task would mean it wouldn't fit before that time runs out then it won't be called."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:316
#: e974fd4387a743b698ef34f81ff96668
msgid "Another point to look at closely is the ins.wait_for_sample() call. That is the \"metronome\" that drives the scheduling in ArduPilot. It blocks execution of the main vehicle thread until a new IMU sample is available. The time between IMU samples is controlled by the arguments to the ins.init() call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:322
#: aaa0f4efd3634815a68f24e8470c129e
msgid "Note that tasks in AP_Scheduler tables must have the following attributes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:325
#: b59e49d4dfee49afa0267cd04c99d387
msgid "they should never block (except for the ins.update() call)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:326
#: cda14c953e0f4618a3ff76542b1c0571
msgid "they should never call sleep functions when flying (an autopilot, like a real pilot, should never sleep while flying)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:328
#: edf193cbc98c4c55b1a6f3149503103f
msgid "they should have predictable worst case timing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:330
#: 598ccff1af7a4645bf0837de9c964669
msgid "You should now go and modify the Scheduler_test example and add in your own tasks to run. Try adding tasks that do the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:333
#: 591de8b3d0e641dca6915ec46a67c1be
msgid "read the barometer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:334
#: 6e17e00e9d5b4614bbd74b7918c246b3
msgid "read the compass"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:335
#: 0460d2e85d24435b93ba66d760918b83
msgid "read the GPS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:336
#: 23e66b51b29a44df9c37ff6e407ca783
msgid "update the AHRS and print the roll/pitch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:338
#: 5a3db3a90bd34f7cb7a59d5ca8d92421
msgid "Look at the example sketches for each library that you worked with earlier in this tutorial to understand how to use each sensor library."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:342
#: 02c42e42e6164c46b407cd87739404a7
msgid "Semaphores"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:344
#: 4bc376cd1cbe4f528878d345195eed3d
msgid "When you have multiple threads (or timer callbacks) you need to ensure that data structures shared by the two logical threads of execution are updated in a way that prevents corruption. There are 3 principle ways of doing this in ArduPilot - semaphores, lockless data structures and the PX4 ORB."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:350
#: ef8eed6ff97e4a928e83a081f438fa2c
msgid "AP_HAL Semaphores are just wrappers around whatever semaphore system is available on the specific platform, and provide a simple mechanism for mutual exclusion. For example, I2C drivers can ask for the I2C bus semaphore to ensure that only one I2C device is used at a time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:355
#: bafe547472e34d399ef2a47a606e39cb
msgid "Go and have a look at the hmc5843 driver in libraries/AP_Compass/AP_Compass_HMC5843.cpp and look for the \\get_semaphore() call. Look at all the places it is used, and see if you can work out why it is needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:361
#: 49dba6f5b5c84c60b2bd3123ec71ed5e
msgid "Lockless Data Structures"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:363
#: 2014a94ea1eb4a8482794db68a943705
msgid "The ArduPilot code also contains examples of using lockless data structures to avoid the need for a semaphore. This can be a lot more efficient than semaphores."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:367
#: ba32d30884b94402985995954c665379
msgid "Two examples of lockless data structures in ArduPilot are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:369
#: 88b6cf0566ac49c3953fea315b03df42
msgid "the \\_shared_data structure in libraries/AP_InertialSensor/AP_InertialSensor_MPU9250.cpp"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:371
#: 9d6b3b153be14e8b80be5faefb62cfba
msgid "the ring buffers used in numerous places. A good example is libraries/DataFlash/DataFlash_File.cpp"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/learning-ardupilot-threading.rst:374
#: b8aaa996e0d54670a799beeff1bd1a74
msgid "Go and have a look at these two examples, and prove to yourself that they are safe for concurrent access. For DataFlash_File look at the use of the \\_writebuf_head and \\_writebuf_tail variables."
msgstr ""

